  => STEP 3 PASS: the DUT opened the connection itself.  swi_b's capture shows
     "10.38.22.97.37982 > 10.38.22.98.23: Flags [S]", a SYN from the DUT, answered [S.] and
     completed with [.].  It is a telnet client and not only a TCP client: it negotiates
     RFC 854 options -- WILL TERMINAL TYPE, "SB TERMINAL TYPE IS 0x76 0x74 0x32 0x32 0x30"
     (vt220), DO SUPPRESS GO AHEAD, WILL NAWS with "SB NAWS IS 0x50 0 0x18" (80 x 24), then
     DO ECHO.  The session is really remote: `show system` run through it returns
     AT-x960-28YSQ, S/N A10737A261700021, awplus_main-20261020-78.  That is a different
     model, a different serial number and a different build train from the DUT.  Both units
     print the host name "awplus", so the serial number is what settles it.  The far end
     authenticated the user and recorded the client's address twice, in `show users`
     ("vty 0 cktmp ... 10.38.22.97") and in its own log ("telnetd[31382]: Connection from
     10.38.22.97 received.").  The session then closed cleanly back to the DUT's own prompt.

